look around  you will be fine  [Music]  that was you'll be found from the hit  Broadway show  dear Evan Hanson sung by fans all over  the world  the musical touches on a difficult topic  but one that deserves our attention the  recent deaths of fashion designer Kate  Spade and celebrity chef Anthony  Bourdain but a focus on the problem of  suicide in this country the Netflix  series thirteen reasons why stirred  controversy when it was blamed for  possibly inspiring suicides but  remarkably dear Evan Hansen's been  credited with the opposite helping fans  cope with issues that can lead to  depression jamie wax is here to explain  Jamie good morning good morning Anthony  less than two years since its Broadway  premiere dear Evan Hansen continues a  radical experiment using a musical to  address the issues of depression and  suicide it's got a chart-topping  original cast album and six Tony Awards  but the show is also hailed for a  realistic and constructive approach to  an issue that ails so many did you  foresee this piece having such a huge  impact when you began your work on it  everybody that we told the idea to  thought that we were insane and was very  polite about it but it just doesn't  sound like a musical and it didn't sound  like something that people thought had  any kind of commercial viability and the  Tony Award goes to  dear Evan Hansen the success of dear  Evan Hansen caught everybody by surprise  including playwright Steven Levinson who  created the musical along with the  oscar-winning songwriting team binge  PASOK and Justin Paul  [Music]  it tells the story of an unlikely bond  between the socially anxious and  isolated high school senior Evan Hansen  [Music]  and the family coping with the suicide  of Evans classmate Connor Murphy it's  anything but your typical Broadway  blockbuster but according to Levinson  what the audience found in this show and  appreciated was a sense of authenticity  I think it's about empowering people and  letting them know that there is help and  that there are people that are there to  listen and as cheesy as that sounds it's  true that message began resonating with  audiences well before its success on  Broadway Rachael Bay Jones received a  Tony for her role as Evans mother Heidi  when we did early productions even early  readings and workshops the people who  came there responded in such an  emotionally visceral way that it was  clear that this is going to make an  impact on a lot of people  [Music]  Taylor Trench currently plays the role  of Evan Hansen I got like piles of  letters every week from people who have  gone through experiences similar to  Evans or to Connors and it is  eye-opening to see people feel safe  writing it and how widespread it is Alex  Boniello plays Connor Murphy whose  character takes his own life at the  beginning of the musical how does  dear Evan Hansen stay so true and in  many ways so sharply brutally honest  about different things and not  glamorized the issue of suicide yeah  it's you know it's so fascinating  because it's something that I've been  thinking a lot about there's a lyric  that my character sings and it's if you  never get around to doing some  remarkable thing that doesn't mean that  you're not worth remembering if you  never get around to doing some  remarkable thing that doesn't mean that  you're not worse remember when we look  at someone like Anthony Bourdain or Kate  Spade these are people who have changed  millions of lives and it still can't  always fix or change whatever is going  on that's causing the the terrible  issues for you but to know that you're  not alone and that your life is worth  something always you don't have to be  extraordinary to be lovable that's right  that's right you know you're not alone  you will be found  [Music]  when the show premiered on Broadway at  the end of 2016  suicide rates were at a 30-year high  it's the second leading cause of death  and people aged 10 to 20 for the fourth  leading cause in those between 35 and 54  while nearly 9% of those 18 to 25  admitted to having suicidal thoughts we  had no idea that the show would start  the conversations that it started and  we're not experts we quickly learned  from the experts what to say and where  to guide people and how to get people to  the right places if they needed help  those early moves to kind of partner  with those people and to get that  conversation started we're very  beneficial to the show and kept us  honest and kept us knowing the  responsibility that we had early on the  show teamed up with a number of advocacy  and suicide prevention groups including  the child mind Institute and the JED  foundation first for guidance with the  script then for ongoing support as an  expert who's worked in this field your  entire life when you first saw the  musical was there something in you that  said yes this story's gonna help people  yes absolutely  I saw how affected the audience was and  it was clear that it was really sort of  speaking to people in a very deep way  dr. Victor Schwartz is the jet  foundation's chief medical officer  they've continued to be directly  involved with the production you've  actually done as an organization a  number of interventions connected to his  show yes we have is it overstating it to  say that dear Evan Hanson has saved  lives  no I don't think so we're at a time  where there have been several  high-profile suicides recently is there  a takeaway in your experience and about  how we should be talking about this so  much of my response is just as a  storyteller we always knew from a pretty  early moment we want the audience to  walk away feeling like these characters  are going to be ok and the most powerful  thing that I remember was  been flat when he was in the show he got  a letter sent to his dressing room  anonymously that said because of you I  didn't let go we get so many thousands  and thousands of letters I'm telling you  that there are so many similarities in  all of them that that one thing alone  has given me more hope strangely  thousands of people writing in saying  they've been touched I felt this way I  did this my family is like this and to  me that's not gives me so much heart and  so much hope because we aren't alone  right interconnectedness is humanity and  if you're in need of that message of  hope but you can't make it to New York  to see the show on Broadway that  grammy-winning cast album is available  wherever you purchase music or stream it  and the show begins its national tour in  Denver in September I love this show I  know you didn't I love this you were  there Ben Platts last night was I've  seen it four times I took each one of my  kids separately to see it it's it is a  brilliant musical with an incredible a  really difficult subject so well bench  passuk and Justin Paul who wrote the  score also wrote greatest showmen learns  in la-la land  incredible just it's incredible show if  you can get to new york get a ticket any  way you can I love it  you know we focus on the message of the  show a lot in this piece but actually  going back and looking at it through the  eyes of this research  it really doesn't cut corners creatively  it's a it doesn't pull punches it deals  with real edge and some brutality with  the issues that it has but that message  of hope and humanity is really central  to other things are so interesting being  in the audience for it is you see the  range of Ages and how it touches  everybody it's a family show in that it  touches you know parents and kids it  really every anybody can relate to it  it's just so brilliant and the team  behind it seems surprised at the amount  of letters they get from people who have  struggled I think they are and I and I  think very early on when the show is  running off-broadway they realized from  the response of people and the fact that  people wanted to stick around and talk  about it that they really needed to do  more powerful stuff Jamie thanks so much  thank you

